The Angular URL discussion is a great example of why precise terminology matters here. Angular's own docs describe this as 'sanitization', but wait... lets look at the implementation.

https://github.com/angular/angular/blob/main/packages/core/src/sanitization/url_sanitizer.ts
_sanitizeUrl in url_sanitizer.ts:
```

const SAFE_URL_PATTERN = /^(?!javascript:)(?:[a-z0-9+.-]+:|[^&:\/?#]*(?:[\/?#]|$))/i;

export function _sanitizeUrl(url: string): string {
  url = String(url);
  if (url.match(SAFE_URL_PATTERN)) return url;

  if (typeof ngDevMode === 'undefined' || ngDevMode) {
    console.warn(`WARNING: sanitizing unsafe URL value ${url} (see ${XSS_SECURITY_URL})`);
  }

  return 'unsafe:' + url;
}

```

A blocklist exclusion for javascript: via negative look ahead combined with an allowlist for valid scheme characters. The function behavior is accept or reject. The entire URL either passes through unchanged or gets neutralized by prepending "unsafe:". No content is selectively modified even though it is labeled as sanitization.
